### Core structure — the tree as prime factorization / CD tower

Every integer passes through 9 CD-tower levels, k=0 (R, leaves, primes) to
k=8 (T_256, root). Composites form a zero-divisor pair at k=4 (sedenion,
first ZD level per Hurwitz 1898: only R/C/H/O are normed division
algebras) and "fall" — their norm fails. A prime has no non-trivial
factorization, so no ZD pair can form, and it reaches T_256 intact. Framed
as the SAME algebraic fact as Fermat's Last Theorem (FLT n>=3 and the ZD
cascade at k=4 are "two languages for one identity"), formally linked via
the N-Shape Theorem in FourthAgePapers' FermatMonster engine (71
holomorphic c=24 VOAs = 71 N-shapes = complete Fermat forbidden zone).

Primes map to sedenion basis elements via p mod 16. The 71 Niemeier
lattices cover only 13 of 16 N-shapes; {e1, e11, e15} are the "Monster
Gap" — algebraically unreachable by regular root systems, filled instead
by the Monster Group. Primes landing there ("silver leaves") are ~35.7%
of primes <=1000. The 5 "Moonshine primes" {17,11,59,31,47} land directly
in the gap.

"The Zeta Index" ζ(p): the index of the first Riemann zero whose
log-space wavelength (λ_n=2π/γ_n) resolves prime p. Each prime gets a
"double index" p_{n[ζ(p)]} — n=ordinal position (π(p)), ζ(p)=spectral
emergence index. Engine: engine/zeta_index_engine.py.

